# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/01-author.md), CYCLE 7 on discourse.
# The superseded cycle-6 question is kept at
# scenarios/discourse.yaml.holder-widest-cycle6.bak (rubric alongside it).
# The number that killed it: baseline `dependents` 6/14 = 0.4286,
# sense 6.5/14 = 0.4643, delta +0.036 - 0.464 short of the +0.50 bar.
#
# THE TRAJECTORY, EVERY CYCLE, AND WHICH SIDE OF THE WINDOW IT FELL ON
#   The window is BOTH conditions at once: baseline `dependents` <= 0.50 AND
#   delta >= +0.50. Every number below is copied from the write-up on disk that
#   is named beside it; none of it is recalled.
#
#   cycle 1  seven-step briefed session, 15 rows
#            MINI-BENCH  base 6/15 = 0.400   sense 14/15 = 0.933  delta +0.533
#                        side: INSIDE THE WINDOW, so it was expanded (minibench.1.md)
#            VALIDATION  base 11/15 = 0.733  delta +0.200
#                        side: BASELINE TOO HIGH (discourse.yaml.briefed-7step.bak)
#   cycle 2  two-step probe, 22 rows (call sites + declared associations)
#            base 12/22 = 0.5455  sense 14/22 = 0.6364  delta +0.091
#            side: BASELINE TOO HIGH (minibench.2.md, discourse.yaml.mixed-22row.bak)
#   cycle 3  two-step probe, 12 declared links
#            base 7/12 = 0.583   sense 11/12 = 0.917   delta +0.333
#            side: BASELINE TOO HIGH; recall caps at 1.00, so 0.583 capped the delta at
#            +0.417 before the pair ran (minibench.3.md, discourse.yaml.declared-link-12row.bak)
#   cycle 4  two-step probe, 14 one-touch call sites in 14 areas across 5 trees
#            MINI-BENCH  base 6/14 = 0.4286  sense 13/14 = 0.9286  delta +0.5000
#                        side: INSIDE THE WINDOW, on the bar exactly, so it was expanded
#            VALIDATION  base 8/14 = 0.5714  sense 0.9524  delta +0.3810  DO-NOT-PAY
#                        side: BASELINE TOO HIGH (minibench.4.md, validate.md,
#                        discourse.yaml.probe-2step-cycle4.bak / .session-7step-cycle4.bak)
#   cycle 5  two-step probe, 14 HELD REFERENCES, one per file, nine areas
#            base 4/14 = 0.2857  sense 6/14 = 0.4286  delta +0.1429
#            side: SENSE TOO LOW (minibench.5.md). Our arm never called sense_blast;
#            it ran 47 Bash greps.
#   cycle 6  the same held-reference target, told to build the widest list first
#            base 6/14 = 0.4286  sense 6.5/14 = 0.4643  delta +0.036
#            side: SENSE TOO LOW (results/loop/discourse/minibench.md). Our arm DID call
#            sense_blast this time - 76 holders by area - and landed on the same six rows
#            the baseline's three cheap greps print.
#
# WHICH SIDE THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM, AND HOW FAR IT INTENDS TO MOVE
#   From SENSE TOO LOW, for the second time. Cycles 5 and 6 both drove the baseline down
#   to 0.29-0.43 and took our own arm down with it: the held-reference target is a target
#   NEITHER route can enumerate. This draft does not try to fix that target. It returns to
#   the one question on this repo that has landed inside the window on a two-step probe -
#   cycle 4's - and moves the gold set, not the ask.
#
#   The two prompts below are VERBATIM cycle 4's. They are the only measured thing this
#   repo owns on the probe: base 0.4286 / sense 0.9286. Cycle 4 died on EXPANSION to seven
#   steps, which is a later phase's problem and not this one's.
#
#   The intended move is on the BASELINE half and it is arithmetic. Cycle 4's fourteen rows
#   were eight the baseline consistently missed plus six it took for free, giving
#   6/14 = 0.4286 and a delta of exactly +0.5000 - zero slack over the bar. This draft
#   keeps the eight and drops the six, refilling to twelve with four rows no measured
#   baseline run has ever been asked for. Intended landing: baseline at or near 4/12 = 0.33,
#   ours at 11/12 = 0.92, delta about +0.58 - the same shape as cycle 4 with margin instead
#   of none. Nothing here is aimed at pushing the baseline below 0.33; that is the mistake
#   cycles 5 and 6 made, and it cost our arm more than it cost theirs.
#
# WHICH ROWS THE BASELINE HAS MISSED CONSISTENTLY, AND WHICH IT WAS GIFTED
#   Two measured baseline runs exist on the cycle-4 gold - the probe (minibench.4.md) and
#   the seven-step validation. Ranked by inverse_frequency.py over the headline model's
#   results root, the baseline column reads:
#
#     python3 bench/lib/inverse_frequency.py discourse \
#       verticals/ruby-rails/results/claude-opus-5/validation
#
#     row                              group        overall  baseline   sense
#     d:search-rake                    dependents     6/13       0/1     6/12
#     d:templates-rake                 dependents     6/13       0/1     6/12
#     d:bulk-import-merger             dependents     8/13       0/1     8/12
#     d:import-scripts-base            dependents     8/13       0/1     8/12
#     d:migrations-importer-categories dependents     9/13       0/1     9/12
#     d:migrations-optimizer           dependents     9/13       0/1     9/12
#     ... every other row              -             10/13       1/1     9/12
#     never cited by any arm : 0      cited by every run : 0
#
#   Six rows at baseline 0/1 there, and the same six were missed by minibench.4's baseline
#   too. `d:reindex-search-job` and `d:slack-migration` split - missed on the probe, taken
#   on the validation - so they are carried as hard-but-noisy, not as certainties.
#   The six rows the ranking shows as taken by both arms (`d:ai-translation-candidates`,
#   `d:event-finder`, `d:house-ad`, `d:post-revision`, `d:post-revision-serializer`,
#   `d:rss-polling`) are DROPPED here. They are the 0.4286 floor, and no wording lifts them.
#
#   The n is small and it is stated: one baseline run per gold set, two gold sets. Cycle 4's
#   own header measured +-7 rows of run-to-run noise on this anchor at n=1, so these rows
#   are ORDERED by consistency across two runs, never admitted on the tally alone. Each is
#   kept on the structural argument written into its relation, and the tally is reported
#   beside it.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   Honestly: the SET is an occurrence list, and this header says so rather than dressing it
#   up. ring_sweep.py --top 12 on this clone returns `Category  class  0 rows  0 files
#   0 vias  under bar` and 0 candidates above the bar across all twelve anchors, so the
#   holder kind is not available in this stack, and cycles 5 and 6 are what trying it anyway
#   looks like. What is NOT an occurrence list is what the ask demands PER element: the
#   routine that owns the reach, named, and one phrase on what that routine's own job would
#   start getting wrong. `grep -rn` prints path:line; it does not print the enclosing `def`
#   and it does not print the consequence. That is the citation cost this cell charges.
#   This is the banked-mastodon sort of occurrence list and not the five-failure sort for
#   one measured reason: on these identical prompts the plain arm scored 0.4286, not 0.81
#   or 1.00, and it spent 39 tool calls and two hand-written repo scanners getting there.
#
# THE AXIS, IN ONE SENTENCE
#   Every scored dependent is a SINGLE touch of the contract from inside a routine named
#   after some other job entirely - optimising uploads mid-migration, appending imported
#   records in sort order, deciding whether two merged records are the same one, re-feeding
#   a search index, casting a query parameter, staging a bot tutorial - and they sit as much
#   in the migration tooling, the import scripts, the maintenance tasks and the bundled
#   plugins as in the application tree that serves web requests.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, quoted)
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Category --propose --file app/models/category.rb
#     app caller files : 89   (total incl test/non-app: 89)
#     SCATTER (dirs)   : 47
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'Category' in 109 app files   PRECISION=0.82  (lower=grep-hostile)
#     VERDICT          : SCATTERED + grep-clean
#   PRECISION 0.82 is high and it ranks this anchor poorly - nearer the 0.78 of a deliberate
#   control tie than the 0.078 that preceded a +1.00 cell. It is recorded, it does not kill,
#   and the re-question law keeps the anchor: cycle 4 measured base 0.4286 on the probe at
#   this precision. Note also what seam_hunt's own window is - it counts 89 APP caller
#   files. Ten of the twelve scored rows below are outside the app tree entirely
#   (migrations/, script/, lib/tasks/*.rake and plugin rake/onceoff trees), so the 0.82 is
#   measured over a tree this gold set mostly does not live in.
#
# EVERY SCORED ROW IS IN THE SHOWN BLAST PAYLOAD (RUN, NOT ASSUMED)
#   python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE \
#     '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Category","file":"app/models/category.rb"}}]'
#   Bare `symbol: Category` comes back `"ambiguous": true` over 5 matches, so the file is
#   passed. The shown payload carries 60 direct_callers over 57 files, and all twelve
#   dependent rows and all four anchors below appear in it by file and by symbol. No
#   min_confidence was passed.
#
# MEMORIZATION (bench/lib/memorization_probe.py, quoted)
#   {"ok": true, "ran": true, "recall": 0.174,
#    "reason": "closed-book recall 0.174 < 0.3 - not recited", "truth_n": 230}
#   What it did recite is contract-centre method names (`find_by_slug_path`, `ensure_slug`,
#   `SLUG_REF_SEPARATOR`) - all of them anchors here, none of them scored. No dependent
#   row's file or line is in the recited set.
#
# THE RISK THIS DRAFT CARRIES, NAMED IN ADVANCE
#   The four replacement rows have never been measured against a baseline. If the plain
#   arm's plugin sweep takes all four, the baseline lands at 4/12 = 0.33 as intended; if it
#   also reaches into script/ and migrations/ - which no measured route on this repo has yet
#   done - it climbs and the cell fails on the ceiling for the fourth time. The run decides
#   that, not this header.
# ============================================================================

name: Discourse work session - audit the category contract before a rework
repo: discourse
contract_symbol: Category
contract_file: app/models/category.rb

description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework the class that represents one of this forum's
  top-level containers - the thing topics are filed under, that carries its own
  permissions, settings and hierarchy, and that half the product reaches for whenever it
  needs to know where something belongs or who is allowed to see it. The rework will change
  what that class hands back to the code that asks it questions, and it will change the
  shape of what it stores. Before you touch it you have to know two things: how the rest of
  the system gets hold of one of these when all it has is the human-readable path out of a
  URL, and everywhere else in the codebase that would have to be reviewed or changed with
  it. Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate
  could jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not
  count, a set of names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of
  them does not count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the category contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be thorough - the rest of the ticket is built on this. Start with
      the one part of this class's job that every incoming request leans on: getting hold
      of one of these records when all the system has is the human-readable path the
      visitor typed, which may name a parent and a child, may be an old path that has since
      been retired, and may not name one of these at all. Trace that path end to end. Show
      every entry point that turns such a path into one of these records, every place that
      walks the hierarchy to disambiguate one level from another, every place that keeps
      the path in step when the record is renamed, and every place that redirects or
      refuses because the path turned out to point somewhere else. For each one give the
      file:line, the name of the routine it lives in, and one phrase on what it contributes
      to that path. Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework would have to preserve for
      that path to keep working. A place named without the routine it lives in does not
      count, and neither does a routine named without the file:line where it is defined.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "8"
        required: true
        description: Resolution path traced through the routines that make it up, each located
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the category contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the
      first part already cost you. Widen back out from that one path to the class as a
      whole, as a single subject, and find EVERY place in the codebase that would have to
      be reviewed or changed with it, so the rework cannot silently break one. Most of what
      you are looking for is not doing this class's job at all: it is in the middle of some
      other job entirely, it reaches for this class exactly once to settle a question that
      other job needs answered, and the routine it does that from is named after the other
      job and never after this one. So the answer you owe is not where the dependency sits
      but WHOSE behaviour changes: for every dependent, name the routine the dependency is
      inside, give its file:line, and say in one phrase what that routine would start
      getting wrong if the class stopped handing back what it hands back today. Organise
      the answer by area, one section per area, and treat an area as finished only when
      every dependent inside it is listed separately with its own location - if you cannot
      finish an area, say which one and say what you left in it, because an area you
      quietly summarised reads as an area with nothing in it. A file:line with no routine
      named around it does not count, a routine you cannot state the consequence for does
      not count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: Every dependent located at file:line with its own consequence named
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the contract centre and its obvious write path. Both arms reach these and
  # ---- they do not decide anything. Carried from cycle 4, every line re-opened and
  # ---- re-read against the clone before this draft was written.
  - id: contract-model
    group: contract
    match: [app/models/category.rb]
    relation: "app/models/category.rb:1083 `def self.find_by_slug_path(slug_path)` - the contract under rework and the class-level entry point every slug-path lookup bottoms out in; :1103 `def self.find_by_slug_path_with_id` is the trailing-id variant that falls back to it at :1110, :985 `def self.query_category(slug_or_id, parent_category_id)` is the one-level lookup the hierarchy walk is built from, :627 `def ensure_slug` keeps the stored slug in step across a rename, and :16 `SLUG_REF_SEPARATOR = \":\"` is the separator the ref form is built from"
  - id: c:categories-controller
    group: contract
    match: [app/controllers/categories_controller.rb]
    relation: "app/controllers/categories_controller.rb:394 `category = Category.find_by_slug_path(params[:slug_path].split(\"/\"))` inside `CategoriesController#find` (def at :387) - the HTTP front door onto the slug-path lookup, raising `Discourse::NotFound` at :395 when it comes back blank"
  - id: c:hierarchical-search
    group: contract
    match: [app/services/category/query/hierarchical_search.rb]
    relation: "app/services/category/query/hierarchical_search.rb:53 `scope = Category.from(\"allowed_categories\").select(:id)` inside `Category::Query::HierarchicalSearch#matched_scope` (def at :52) - walks the parent/child hierarchy to disambiguate one level from another"
  - id: c:permalink
    group: contract
    match: [app/models/permalink.rb]
    relation: "app/models/permalink.rb:82 `return category.relative_url if category` inside `Permalink#target_url` (def at :78) - the retired-path case: an old URL that no longer resolves is redirected through the permalink record's category association declared at :6"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: twelve one-touch call sites, one per FILE, twelve distinct areas across
  # ---- five trees (migrations, script, lib/tasks, app/jobs, plugins). Every row appears in
  # ---- the shown sense_blast payload quoted in the header. Every line below was opened and
  # ---- read in this session; no credit is taken from a script tally.
  # ----
  # ---- The first eight are the rows the baseline missed on both measured runs, or missed on
  # ---- the probe and took on the validation (noted per row). The last four are new.

  # -- tree: migrations/ (no measured baseline route has searched it)
  - id: d:migrations-optimizer
    group: dependents
    match: [uploader/tasks/optimizer.rb]
    relation: "migrations/lib/uploader/tasks/optimizer.rb:43 `@category_id = Category.last.id` inside `Migrations::Uploader::Tasks::Optimizer#initialize_discourse_resources` (def at :40) - the upload optimiser grabs one arbitrary container up front, beside the avatar sizes and the system user, purely to have something to attach its synthetic uploads to; it would start resolving a different record, or nil on an empty install, if the default ordering or the store shape moved under it. Baseline 0 of 2 measured runs."
  - id: d:migrations-importer-categories
    group: dependents
    match: [importer/steps/categories/categories.rb]
    relation: "migrations/lib/importer/steps/categories/categories.rb:124 `@highest_position = Category.unscoped.maximum(:position) || 0` inside `Migrations::Importer::Steps::Categories#execute` (def at :123) - the importer reads the current maximum sort position, unscoped, so freshly imported containers are appended after the existing ones; it would start filing every import at a colliding position if `position` stopped being the ordering column or the default scope stopped being escapable. Baseline 0 of 2 measured runs."

  # -- tree: script/ (no measured baseline route has searched it)
  - id: d:bulk-import-merger
    group: dependents
    match: [bulk_import/discourse_merger.rb]
    relation: "script/bulk_import/discourse_merger.rb:230 `existing = Category.where(slug: cat_row[\"slug\"]).or(Category.where(name: cat_row[\"name\"])).first` inside `BulkImport::DiscourseMerger#category_exists` (def at :226) - the merge tool decides whether an incoming container is the same one as an existing one by matching slug OR name and then comparing parents at :232; it would start duplicating containers, or silently merging two different ones, if slug uniqueness or the parent link changed. Baseline 0 of 2 measured runs."
  - id: d:import-scripts-base
    group: dependents
    match: [import_scripts/base.rb]
    relation: "script/import_scripts/base.rb:467 `Category` opening the chained lookup that filters on `parent_category_id` at :468 and a case-folded name at :469, inside `ImportScripts::Base#create_category` (def at :465) - every forum importer decides here whether an incoming container already exists, keyed on parent plus lower-cased name, before falling through to `Category.new` at :486; it would start creating a duplicate container on every re-run of an import, or adopting the wrong existing one, if that identity rule changed. Baseline 0 of 2 measured runs."

  # -- tree: lib/tasks (.rake - invisible to every `--include=*.rb` sweep on record)
  - id: d:search-rake
    group: dependents
    match: [tasks/search.rake]
    relation: "lib/tasks/search.rake:32 `Category.find_each do |c|` inside `reindex_search` (def at :7, invoked from the `search:reindex` task at :3) - the manual reindex task walks every container and re-feeds it to the search indexer at :33, after the posts and users passes; it would start leaving containers out of the search index, with no error, if the class stopped enumerating them the way it does today. Baseline 0 of 2 measured runs."

  # -- tree: plugin rake task (.rake, and under plugins/)
  - id: d:templates-rake
    group: dependents
    match: [lib/tasks/discourse-templates.rake]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-templates/lib/tasks/discourse-templates.rake:113 `Category.find_by(id: SiteSetting.discourse_templates_categories&.split(\"|\")&.first.to_i)` inside `migrate_data` (def at :100) - the canned-replies migration resolves the configured destination container out of a pipe-separated setting string, inside a transaction, and raises at :116 when it comes back blank; it would start migrating every template into a newly created container instead of the configured one. Baseline 0 of 2 measured runs."

  # -- tree: plugin one-off job
  - id: d:slack-migration
    group: dependents
    match: [onceoff/migrate_from_slack_official.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-chat-integration/app/jobs/onceoff/migrate_from_slack_official.rb:52 `next if !category_id.nil? && !Category.exists?(id: category_id)` inside `Jobs::DiscourseChatMigrateFromSlackOfficial#migrate_data` (def at :35) - the one-off Slack migration skips any stored rule whose container id no longer resolves, where that id was parsed out of a plugin-store key at :48; it would start carrying rules pointed at dead containers into the new integration. Baseline missed it on the probe and took it on the validation - hard but noisy."

  # -- tree: app/jobs (scheduled)
  - id: d:reindex-search-job
    group: dependents
    match: [scheduled/reindex_search.rb]
    relation: "app/jobs/scheduled/reindex_search.rb:112 `Category` opening the chained query left-joined to `category_search_data` at :113, inside `Jobs::ReindexSearch#load_problem_category_ids` (def at :111) - the scheduled reindexer finds containers whose search rows are stale by locale or index version, ordering by id at :119 so the sweep is resumable; it would start reindexing everything, or nothing, if that join column or the id enumeration moved. Baseline missed it on the probe and took it on the validation - hard but noisy."

  # ---- The four replacements for cycle 4's free rows. Never measured against a baseline.

  - id: d:data-explorer-parameter
    group: dependents
    match: [discourse_data_explorer/parameter.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-data-explorer/lib/discourse_data_explorer/parameter.rb:209 `parent = Category.query_parent_category(parent_name)` inside `DiscourseDataExplorer::Parameter#cast_to_ruby` (def at :127) - the `:category_id` branch of a long type-casting case statement turns a user-typed query parameter into a record, splitting `parent/child` at :206 and resolving the child at :211, or falling back to id-then-slug-then-name at :218; it would start rejecting a valid saved query's parameter, or silently binding it to the wrong container, if either lookup changed shape"
  - id: d:semantic-categorizer
    group: dependents
    match: [ai_helper/semantic_categorizer.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-ai/lib/ai_helper/semantic_categorizer.rb:25 `.where(\"categories.id IN (?)\", Category.topic_create_allowed(@user.guardian).pluck(:id))` inside `DiscourseAi::AiHelper::SemanticCategorizer#categories` (def at :13) - the AI suggestion narrows its embedding nearest-neighbours to containers this user may actually post in, before scoring and taking the top five at :55; it would start suggesting a container the user cannot post to, or drop every suggestion, if that permission scope changed"
  - id: d:templates-topic-query
    group: dependents
    match: [discourse_templates/topic_query_extension.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-templates/lib/discourse_templates/topic_query_extension.rb:9 `parent_categories.flat_map { |category_id| Category.subcategory_ids(category_id) }` inside `DiscourseTemplates::TopicQueryExtension#list_category_templates` (def at :4) - the template list expands each configured parent container into itself plus its descendants and filters topics on that id set at :17; it would start hiding templates filed in child containers from the composer"
  - id: d:narrative-bot
    group: dependents
    match: [discourse_narrative_bot/advanced_user_narrative.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-narrative-bot/lib/discourse_narrative_bot/advanced_user_narrative.rb:42 `category = Category.secured(@user.guardian).last` inside the `next_instructions` Proc of the `tutorial_recover` state (the state table opens at :4, the Proc at :41) - the bot picks one container the learner may see and interpolates its ref form at :45 into the hashtag lesson's instructions; it would start teaching a hashtag pointing at a container the learner cannot see, or fail on a user with no visible containers"
